---
layout: null
---
{%- comment -%}
  Search index for the blog listing, rendered by Jekyll to /blog/search.json on every build.

  Consumer: blog/search.js fetches this file once per page view and reads it with JSON.parse
  only; it never writes any of it into the page as HTML.

  Shape: one JSON array over site.posts, newest first (Jekyll's order). search.js uses the
  array position as its tie-break order, so the order must not be changed here. Each entry
  has exactly six keys, in this order: url, title, summary, tags, date, body. Every value
  passes through jsonify (not escape), so titles such as  Escaping "quotes" & <angle>  stay
  raw text inside valid JSON strings.

  url is post.url passed through relative_url, the same string blog/index.html writes into
  each listing item's data-url attribute; search.js joins index entries to listing items on
  it, and it carries the base path in both deployment modes.

  No filtering happens here. In every build without --drafts (GitHub Pages, the CI build and
  local builds) drafts never reach site.posts. The local --drafts preview, and the fixture
  builder's preview variant, do add drafts to site.posts and so to this index; that is
  acceptable because the preview is served only on the author's machine and neither output
  is published. Future-dated posts are left out of every build, the preview included, by
  future: false in _config.yml.

  body is the full rendered article text, code samples included:
    1. strip_html removes the markup but keeps the character references kramdown emits.
    2. The references are decoded: lt, gt, quot and #39 first, amp LAST, so literal entity
       text shown in a code sample (double-encoded in the HTML) comes out as written, and a
       body-only R&D is indexed as r&d rather than r&amp;d.
    3. Whitespace is collapsed and the text lowercased.
    4. split, uniq and join reduce the text to its distinct tokens in first-occurrence order.
       Query terms never contain whitespace and ranking counts which fields contain a term,
       not how often, so this changes no search result and roughly halves the index.

  This block renders nothing, and its whitespace control keeps the output starting with the
  opening bracket. Keep braces out of this comment, and add no HTML or JSON comments below:
  either would make the output invalid JSON. With no published articles the output is [].
{%- endcomment -%}
[{% for post in site.posts %}{% unless forloop.first %},{% endunless %}
{"url":{{ post.url | relative_url | jsonify }},"title":{{ post.title | jsonify }},"summary":{{ post.summary | jsonify }},"tags":{{ post.tags | jsonify }},"date":{{ post.date | date_to_xmlschema | jsonify }},"body":{{ post.content | strip_html | replace: "&lt;", "<" | replace: "&gt;", ">" | replace: "&quot;", '"' | replace: "&#39;", "'" | replace: "&amp;", "&" | normalize_whitespace | downcase | split: " " | uniq | join: " " | jsonify }}}{% endfor %}]
